[b]Elder Ray Pressure Engine[/b]

[b]What it does[/b]

A research framework built on Alexander Elder's Bull Power / Bear Power concept — the maximum buyer/seller excursion against a consensus-of-value line. Instead of committing to one fixed formula, it exposes four distinct pressure engines, six consensus lines, and five normalization modes as independent switches, so each can be compared against real price action rather than assumed correct.

[b]Pressure engines[/b]
[list]
[*][b]Classic Elder[/b] — High/Low against consensus, the original definition; both sides can be positive or negative together when the whole bar trades on one side of the line
[*][b]Directional Elder[/b] — excursion clamped to its own side only, removing that ambiguity from the pressure reading
[*][b]True Range Elder[/b] — gap-aware, includes the previous close in the extremes
[*][b]Close-Weighted Elder[/b] — directional excursion scaled by where the bar actually closed within its range
[/list]

[b]Consensus and normalization[/b]

The reference line (EMA / RMA / SMA / WMA / HMA / VWMA, any source) and the scaling (Raw / ATR / Average Range / Percent of consensus / RMS) are independent inputs, so a pressure definition, its reference, and its units can each be tested on their own.

[b]Exhaustion vs. extreme[/b]

Extreme pressure (top/bottom percentile over a lookback window) is not treated as a signal by itself — it usually just means a strong, healthy move. Exhaustion requires the pressure to already be fading while price is still pushing to new highs/lows — the move losing the backing that produced it. Extreme recency and the price-push comparison use independent windows. An outside bar satisfying both directions is classified as Dual Exhaustion.

[b]Divergence[/b]

Bar-close-confirmed pivot divergences between price and Bull/Bear Power: a higher price high with a lower Bull Power high (bearish), or a lower price low with a higher Bear Power low (bullish). Markers appear on the bar where the pivot becomes confirmed, not retrospectively on the earlier pivot bar.

[b]Signal processing[/b]

Pressure impulse and acceleration can be displayed independently. Dominance is a signed share of net versus gross pressure, so its -1 to +1 meaning remains consistent across all four calculation models.

[b]Position[/b]

A separate Above / Below / Straddle readout restores the whole-bar-vs-consensus information that Directional and Close-Weighted Elder deliberately remove from the pressure values themselves.

[b]Dashboard[/b]

A top-right table shows the active calculation model, regime, bar position, pressure state, Bull/Bear/Net/Gross pressure, dominance, and both percentile ranks.

[b]Scope[/b]

This indicator deliberately stops at pressure, exhaustion, and divergence — no composite score, no long/short signal. It is meant to establish which engine/consensus/normalization combination actually discriminates before any decision layer is built on top of it.

